speechtext:
My hon. friend over here has taken up a great deal of the time of the house, and whenever he makes a speech he asks for a little extra time. I do not take up very much time, and I try to talk reasonably and sensibly when I am addressing the house. I have been obliged to listen to a great deal of bunk, and I have listened without interfering with the hon. member; I have let him go on with it.
Let me say here that we are getting off on the wrong foot in this country when we bring about any differences between capital and labour. They are not enemies; they are partners in the development of this dominion, and you could not place upon the statute books any law that would reflect upon one that would not work back to the other. We must stand or fall together in this country;

we must work together for its development. Industry looks to the farmer for a market, and the farmer produces the food for industry. Each is dependent upon the other, and we must cease flying at each other's throats. I am confident that if we were manufacturing in Canada the goods that we consume in this country, we would give employment to every man and woman here, and to a great many more men and women who would be very pleased to make this the land of their adoption. We have listened long enough to this cry of free trade, and I think brighter days are coming. When two men, elected as Liberals, such as the hon. member for Brantford City and the hon. member for Renfrew South rise in their places and make such speeches as we have heard to-day, the future does not look so dark.
